import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Exam Technique: The 10-mark Decision Question">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/ibbm/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <a href="/ibbm/examtechnique/">Exam Technique</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <span>The 10-mark Decision Question</span>
  </div>
  <div class="topic-hero">
    <span class="topic-number">Exam Technique</span>
    <h1>The 10-mark Decision Question</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Questions that ask you to recommend or decide between two named options, on Paper 1 Section B and Paper 2 Section B.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="learning-goals">
    <div class="learning-goals-title">Applies to</div>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/ibbm/unit5/5-2/">5.2 Operations Methods</a> — changing production method</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="tabs">
    <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="switchTab(this,'notes')">Notes</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'practice')">Practice</button>
  </div>

  <!-- NOTES -->
  <div id="notes" class="tab-content active">

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Same question, two papers</h2>
      <p>Questions that ask you to recommend or decide between two named options (like "should Kettleworks change from mass/flow to mass customisation?") turn up on both Paper 1 Section B and Paper 2 Section B. They reward a specific structure.</p>

      <div class="callout key-term">
        <div class="callout-title">Same, on both papers</div>
        <p>Command terms are similar (discuss, discuss whether, explain whether, recommend whether). You need balance and application to the stimulus, and you need to name a limitation of the stimulus for top marks.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout warning">
        <div class="callout-title">Different: how you must finish</div>
        <p>On Paper 1 Section B, a balanced answer can score above 5/10 without picking a side. On Paper 2's 10-mark question, you must reach a <strong>justified decision between two named options</strong>: every real example of this question is framed as a choice, and the mark scheme expects you to make one.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Structure a 10-mark "should they do A or B" answer like this:</p>
      <div class="content-card">
        <div class="content-card-title">Option A / Option B structure</div>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Option A:</strong> describe it, then 1–2 advantages and 1–2 disadvantages, each tied to the stimulus</li>
          <li><strong>Option B:</strong> the same treatment</li>
          <li><strong>Compare and conclusion, written last:</strong> weigh the two options against each other using this specific company's situation. Take a position</li>
          <li><strong>Limitations of the stimulus:</strong> what's missing or uncertain, and how knowing it would change or reinforce the decision</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-muted);">The compare/conclusion and limitations steps can swap order depending on what works for you, but the conclusion always comes last.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">What a good conclusion does</div>
        <p>It comes last and never states the answer first. It weighs the strongest points against each other: "On balance, [Company] should choose Option B because ___, although Option A offers [strength], it matters less here because ___." It ties back to this company's specific situation (cash, risk, size, timeframe). It references the limitation you named: what would confirm or change your decision. And it takes a real position, even a close one.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Common weaknesses, from examiner reports</h2>
      <p>Subject reports on questions like this one keep flagging the same two failure modes. Both lose marks even when the content is factually correct.</p>

      <div class="callout warning">
        <div class="callout-title">Context without theory</div>
        <p>Copying a fact from the stimulus without explaining the business mechanism behind it earns no knowledge marks. For example, a report on a question about converting from a private to a public limited company noted that some students wrote only that the advantage is "they can raise finance for expansion" — lifting the phrase straight from the case. That's not explanation: knowledge is only credited once the student explains <em>how</em>, e.g. that a public limited company can attract new shareholders by selling shares on a stock exchange through an IPO.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout warning">
        <div class="callout-title">Theory without context</div>
        <p>The opposite failure: writing a generic, textbook-level point that could apply to any company, with no link back to the stimulus. The same report gave "possible loss of control" as an example — true, and worth one mark, but the second mark needs the point contextualised to this specific case (the report's example: naming the case's mention of "hostile takeovers of smaller businesses").</p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">The pattern</div>
        <p>Business management is an applied subject. Every point needs both halves: the business theory (why this happens, in general) and the business practice (what in <em>this</em> stimulus shows it). Reports on this question type repeatedly make the same recommendation: balance theory with case-specific application, every time.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="content-card">
        <div class="content-card-title">Bridging the two: same point, three ways</div>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Using Kettleworks (5.2) as an example — the point being made is "switching to mass customisation lets Kettleworks compete on personalisation."</p>
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li><strong>Context only (weak):</strong> "Competitors let customers choose colour, handle style and engraving, so Kettleworks should do the same."</li>
          <li><strong>Theory only (weak):</strong> "Mass customisation lets a business offer personalisation at close to mass-production cost, which can differentiate it from competitors."</li>
          <li><strong>Bridged (strong):</strong> "Because rivals already let customers personalise their kettles without charging much more, Kettleworks' current single white design is now a weakness rather than a cost advantage. Mass customisation would let it match that personalisation using the existing automated line with swappable options, restoring the differentiation it has lost — provided its strong cash reserves can absorb the switch-over cost."</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /notes -->

  <!-- PRACTICE -->
  <div id="practice" class="tab-content">

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Checklist your Kettleworks answer</div>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Go back to the answer you wrote for <a href="/ibbm/unit5/5-2/">5.2, Activity 5 (Kettleworks Ltd)</a>. Tick each box your answer does, then note how you could improve it in five minutes.</p>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Checklist: Kettleworks</div>
        <ul>
          <li>Uses specific stimulus detail, not generic knowledge</li>
          <li>One point in favour of staying with mass/flow</li>
          <li>One point against staying with mass/flow</li>
          <li>One point in favour of switching to mass customisation</li>
          <li>One point against switching to mass customisation</li>
          <li>More than one business function considered (HR, finance, marketing)</li>
          <li>Specific business terminology and/or BMTs used</li>
          <li>Balanced, not one-sided</li>
          <li>Names a stimulus limitation and its effect on the decision</li>
          <li>Ends with real synthesis, not a summary</li>
          <li>Key terms used accurately</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Practice, Paper 2 style — Voltway Bikes</div>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">
        <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1l90IGVT-B_38W8oOXfZHvIBKQCRTuDgsRoy9NGXGAd8/edit?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Voltway Bikes Ltd, Paper 2 Section B Practice</a>.
        Questions 1 to 6 are a finance and marketing review in short answers (10 minutes). Question 7 is the 10-mark question, using the same Option A / Option B / Compare / Limitations structure as above (20 minutes).
      </p>
      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">Before you write Q7</div>
        <p>Plan using Option A / Option B / Limitations / Compare before you start writing. Once you've written it, go back to the checklist above and mark your own answer against it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /practice -->

